#use(std/code)
#use(std/math: asciimath)

#section{Math}::

#code{#use(std/math: asciimath)} binds the math plugin to AsciiMath notation
(the variant chooses the notation; the core knows nothing about math)
#code|{#m{…}}| is inline math, #code{#math} + indented block is display
math; both render to MathML

#code(lang: "plain")
	The identity #m{e^(i pi) + 1 = 0} is famous.

	#math
		x = (-b +- sqrt(b^2 - 4a c)) / (2a)

Live:

The identity #m{e^(i pi) + 1 = 0} is famous

#math
	x = (-b +- sqrt(b^2 - 4a c)) / (2a)

The supported AsciiMath subset includes fractions (#code{a/b}), sub/super
scripts (#code{x_i^2}), roots (#code{sqrt(x)}, #code{root(3)(x)}), greek
names (#code{alpha}, #code{pi}, #code{Gamma}), and big operators
(#code{sum_(i=0)^n}):

Live: #m{sum_(i=0)^n i^2}, #m{root(3)(x) + sqrt(x^2+1)}, #m{alpha + beta = Gamma - pi}

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: #code{#m} is the only inline form — there is deliberately no reader-macro shorthand like #code{$…$}
	#item: The body is a custom reader: braces must balance, but #code{#} and markup are not interpreted inside
	#item: Unknown symbols degrade gracefully — #m{a ? b} keeps the #code{?} as an operator; nothing errors on unrecognized input
	#item: Both forms require the plugin; without #code{#use(std/math: asciimath)} the names #code{m}/#code{math} are unbound
